% SLIDE 4. 05-08. The grammar specifies grouping, not direct parser calls.
% Implement left-recursive arithmetic rules with loops in recursive descent.
### Expression Grammar for This Project
[grammar] @(70,150) {$code, w=1140px, size=25px} ```text
expression  -> assignment
assignment  -> primary ('=' assignment)?
comparison  -> sum (COMPARE_OP sum)?
sum         -> sum [+-] product | product
product     -> product [*/] unary | unary
unary       -> [!-] unary | primary
primary     -> NUMBER | ID | '(' expression ')'
```
...
[assoc] @(70,500) {$note} **sum and product are left-associative.** Implement these left-recursive rules with loops that grow the left subtree. Assignment recurses right. Comparison permits one operator.
...
[check] @(70,640) {$note, size=25px, color=focus} After parsing an assignment, check that its left subtree is a Variable node.
---
% SLIDE 5. 08-11. Walk tokens through the two recursive calls.
### Right-Associative Assignment
[source] @(70,150) {$code, w=700px, size=27px} ```text
a = b = 7

Assignment
  Variable(a)
  Assignment
    Variable(b)
    Number(7)
```
...
[parse] @(835,155) {$note, w=365px} Parse the left comparison. On `=`, parse another assignment as the right child.
...
[shape] @(835,340) {$note, w=365px, color=emp} The tree means `a = (b = 7)`. Its target is a variable binding, not a value to load.
...
[contrast] @(70,500) {$note} `12 / 3 / 2` groups as `(12 / 3) / 2`, yielding 2. Arithmetic uses a loop that repeatedly extends the left subtree.
...
[api] @(70,625) {$note, size=25px, color=id} `ASTKind::Assignment` children: target, then value. `AddChild` owns each subtree by value.
---
% SLIDE 6. 11-14. Beware blindly reusing a generic binary-operator loop.
### Comparison Syntax and Assignment Targets
[examples] @(70,155) {$code, w=750px, size=26px} ```cpp
// INVALID:
a < b < c;
a == b != c;
(a + b) = 7;

// Legal when a, b, c are declared:
(a < b) == 1;
(a) = 7;
```
[compare] @(865,165) {$note, w=335px} After one comparison, reject another comparison operator at this level.
...
[parens] @(865,340) {$note, w=335px, color=emp} Parentheses start a new expression parse. They can return the enclosed AST directly.
...
[target] @(70,545) {$note} An assignment target must resolve to a Variable node. Parenthesized `a` still identifies a variable. The sum `a + b` does not.
[where] @(70,645) {$note, size=24px, color=id} Target checks fit the semantic visitor. Chained-comparison rejection belongs in parsing.
---
% SLIDE 7. 14-17. Ask students to execute this using Strix rules, not C++ rules.
### Evaluation Order Is Observable
[source] @(70,150) {$code, w=750px, size=26px} ```cpp
function LeftRead() : double {
  double x = 10;
  return x - (x = 2);
}
```
[predict] @(865,165) {$note, w=335px, color=focus} What is the returned value? What would reverse-order evaluation return?
...
[trace] @(70,350) {$code, w=750px, size=25px} ```text
left operand:   load x, retain 10
right operand:  store x = 2, retain 2
subtraction:   10 - 2 = 8
```
...
[answer] @(865,380) {$note, w=335px, color=emp} Required: 8. Reading x after the assignment would instead yield 2 - 2 = 0.
...
[rule] @(70,570) {$note} Strix evaluates operands left to right. Associativity controls grouping. Evaluation order controls when effects happen.
---
% SLIDE 8. 17-20. Code is a function-body fragment, not a complete module.
% Wasm local.tee semantics: spec/core/exec/instructions.html#exec-local-tee.
### Assignment Translation Preserves Its Value
[wat] @(70,150) {$code, w=750px, size=26px} ```wat
;; a = b = 7
;; a is $var0, b is $var1
(f64.const 7)
(local.tee $var1)
(local.tee $var0)
```
[stack] @(865,155) {$note, w=335px} `local.tee` copies the top stack value into a local variable while leaving the operand stack unchanged.
...
[stmt] @(70,390) {$code, w=750px, size=26px} ```wat
;; If used as the statement a = b = 7;
(drop)
```
...
[expr] @(865,380) {$note, w=335px, color=emp} Note: If used inside return or arithmetic, keep the value for the enclosing expression.
...
[contract] @(70,540) {$note} {{color=explain|Declaration}}: initializer plus `local.set`.{{br}}{{color=explain|Assignment expression}}: right side plus `local.tee`. {{br}}{{color=explain|Expression statement}}: expression plus `drop`.
---
% SLIDE 9. 20-23. Explicit statements avoid C++ string-concatenation order issues.
### A Visitor Preserves the Source Contract
[pseudo] @(70,150) {$code, w=790px, size=24px} ```text
GenerateExpression(Binary node):
    GenerateExpression(left child)
    GenerateExpression(right child)
    GenerateOperation(opcode)

GenerateExpression(Assignment node):
    GenerateExpression(right child)
    id = left child's resolved variable_id
    Generate("local.tee", id)
```
[why] @(905,160) {$note, w=295px} Emit the left operand's code before the right operand's code. Execution then follows the required order.
...
[when] @(905,380) {$note, w=295px, color=focus} The compiler emits instructions. Each Wasm call executes them.
...
[ast] @(70,580) {$note, size=27px, color=id} This is pseudocode. In the scaffold, inspect `const ASTNode &`, use `GetChild`, and read each node's resolved IDs. Keep translation separate from parsing.
---
% SLIDE 10. 23-25. Short discussion, not another full stack lecture.
### A Stack Audit for Any New Node
[expr] @(70,155) {$note} **{{color=explain|Expression}}:** leaves one f64 above whatever was already on the stack. Include assignment, comparison, and `!`.
...
[stmt] @(70,280) {$note} **{{color=explain|Statement}}:** leaves the existing stack unchanged when it completes normally. `return`, break, and continue transfer control.
...
[compare] @(70,405) {$note} A {{color=explain|comparison}} first yields i32. Append `f64.convert_i32_s` when it produces a Strix value. A condition compares its f64 value with zero to obtain i32.
...
[bug] @(70,555) {$note, color=lightyellow} {{color=red|Note}}: A bare expression statement that forgets `drop` can invalidate a function even if its return expression is correct.
---
% SLIDE 11. 25-28. Ask which x each reference denotes before assigning IDs.
### A Source Name Can Denote Several Locals
[source] @(70,150) {$code, w=750px, size=24px} ```cpp
function ScopeExample() : double {
  double x = 10;
  {
    double x = x + 1;
    x = 20;
  }
  return x;
}
```
[bindings] @(865,155) {$note, w=335px} Outer x gets ID 0. Inner x gets ID 1. The initializer reads ID 0. The assignment writes ID 1.

@(865,355) {$note, w=435px} {{color=emp|What does the function return?}}
...
[result] @(865,420) {$note, w=435px, color=emp} **{{size=40|10}}** - Leaving the inner scope restores the outer scope.
...
[identity] @(70,580) {$note} A scope map answers which declaration a name means here. `Program::variables` retains every declaration for later phases.
