% SLIDE 22. 57-60. Begin the 18-minute application block.
### Building Challenge: GCD in Strix
...
[source] @(70,145) {$code, w=790px, size=23px} ```cpp
function GCD(double a, double b) : double {
  if (a == 0) return b;
  if (b == 0) return a;
  while (a != b) {
    if (a > b) a = a - b;
    else b = b - a;
  }
  return a;
}
```
[domain] @(905,155) {$note, w=295px} Example domain: small nonnegative integers, stored as doubles. Handle zero first.
...
[algorithm] @(905,355) {$note, w=295px, color=emp} Subtract the smaller positive value from the larger. Stop when they match.
---
% SLIDE 23. 60-63. Have pairs trace one iteration each, then show the tests.
### GCD Trace and Progress
(a, b)
...
(48, 18)
...
(30, 18)
...
(12, 18)
...
(12,  6)
...
( 6,  6)  => return 6

[proof] @(665,155) {$note, w=535px} For positive integers, subtracting the smaller preserves the common divisors and reduces a + b.
...
[zero] @(665,300) {$note, w=535px, color=focus} Without the early returns, what happens to GCD(0, 5)?
...
[answer] @(665,410) {$note, w=535px, color=focus} It would repeatedly compute 5 - 0. The explicit zero case prevents that infinite loop.
[cases] @(70,630) {$note, size=26px, color=emp} Some other tests (48,18): 6, (18,48): 6, (7,7): 7, (0,5): 5, (5,0): 5, (0,0): 0.
