% SLIDE 24. 63-68. Live: type module, export, guards, loop condition.
% No live coding: students write the condition/exit sequence in pairs.
### GCD Workshop: Loop Entry and Exit
[wat] @(70,150) {$code, w=750px, size=24px} ```wat
;; a is $a, b is $b, both f64 parameters
;; After handling zero inputs:
(block $exit
  (loop $again
    (local.get $a)
    (local.get $b)
    (f64.ne)          ;; i32: a != b
    (i32.eqz)         ;; Are they equal?
    (br_if $exit)
    ;; Body goes here
    (br $again)
  )
)
```
[reason] @(865,155) {$note, w=335px} The comparison feeds a condition directly here, so it can remain i32 in this handwritten WAT.
...
[generic] @(865,380) {$note, w=335px, color=emp} Your general compiler may convert to a source f64 and back. Correctness comes before optimization.
---
% SLIDE 25. 68-72. Live: fill the body; ask where each assignment value goes.
% No live coding: half the room writes the then branch, half writes the else.
### GCD Workshop: Updates and Return
[wat] @(70,145) {$code, w=790px, size=24px} ```wat
;; Inside the loop, before (br $again):
(local.get $a)
(local.get $b)
(f64.gt)
(if
  (then
    (local.set $a
      (f64.sub (local.get $a) (local.get $b))))
  (else
    (local.set $b
      (f64.sub (local.get $b) (local.get $a))))
)
;; After the block:
(return (local.get $a))
```
[set] @(905,155) {$note, w=295px} Each assignment is a statement. This handwritten version uses local.set because it discards the value.
...
[generic] @(905,390) {$note, w=295px, color=emp} A compiler may use local.tee followed by drop. Both preserve the source behavior.
---
